<p><em>Goliath of the Groundstruck, from Ulearos (Lone Peak). Circle of the Stars druid.</em></p>
<h3>Who he is</h3>
<p>Talo was born on Ulearos, the mountain that was once a flying city, among the goliaths who believe they were stars scraped from the sky when it fell. Every Groundstruck has a star of their own, and a lifetime is spent learning which one it is. To die is only to <em>know the sky once again</em>.</p>
<h3>His brother</h3>
<p>Talo was teaching his younger brother to fly, the way the Groundstruck learn: by watching, and then by jumping. The boy was close, and Talo told him honestly, <em>"You're ready. Tomorrow, I'll be there."</em> The next day Talo was held up. His brother grew impatient and jumped alone to surprise him. The wind turned, and his shift failed.</p>
<p>His brother was young and had not yet found his star. To the Groundstruck that is the worst death there is: a soul that may be lost between the sky and the world, unable to go home.</p>
<p>The druids held their trial and found no fault. The boy was loved and deeply tied to the world, so they judged that he would be reborn. It was an accident, and no one blamed Talo. His family did not blame him.</p>
<p>Talo did. He replayed his words and the place he had promised to be, and the kindness of everyone around him was harder to bear than blame would have been.</p>
<h3>The sentence</h3>
<p>Among his people, the punishment for killing a goliath before they find their star is to be locked away from the sky, underground, for years. The druids would not give Talo that sentence, so he gave it to himself. He walked down off the mountain against his people's wishes and into the Underdark, and he stopped looking for his star.</p>
<p>He spent many years there. He learned to live in the deep, and in time he blended into drow society on its lowest rungs: never mistaken for a drow and thought little of, but treated as one of the locals. Through all of it his star magic dimmed but never left him. Slowly he came to see that as the sky refusing to give up on him.</p>
<h3>Now</h3>
<p>Talo has served his sentence and come to terms with what happened. The guilt has not gone; he has decided to carry it home. Leaving the Underdark also cost his family a second son, and he means to make that right.</p>
<p>The deep he knows well. The way up he had long forgotten. Hidden in the middle of the Underdark, he could go unseen for years, but the drow watch the routes to the surface closely. Lost in unfamiliar tunnels on his way out, he was picked up by a drow patrol.</p>
<p>The drow do not treat him as an enemy of the house. To them he is a local whose papers have lapsed: held in a holding chamber, unchained, until they sort out what he is and where he belongs. His gear has been taken.</p>
<h3>How he looks</h3>
<p>A huge goliath, about 7&frac12; feet tall, in his late thirties, healthy and powerful. His skin is grey and stone-textured, scattered with tiny pale star-flecks laid out in faint constellations across his shoulders and arms, the mark of the Groundstruck, usually dulled under cave dust. His head is shaved, his dark beard is thick and kept neat, and his pale grey eyes are narrowed in a habitual squint against light.</p>
<p>He dresses as an Underdark commoner in drow-made clothes: a dark spider-silk tunic, a worn rothé-leather vest and silk wraps on his forearms, with nothing about him that says he came from the surface. Tucked under the wrap at one wrist is a small grey bird feather bound in silk thread.</p>`;
