 { marker:"HW1Pp5gYtwMofryI", folder:fC, uuid:"Compendium.dnd-monster-manual.actors.Actor.mmWarriorVeteran", name:"Dugbor", art:T+"dwarves/dwarf-negotiator.png", ring:RS+"dwarf-negotiator-434b95.webp", dv:120, sub:"Dwarf", size:"med", attrs:["garhammartradeleague"],
   bio:`<p><strong>Dugbor</strong> (DUG-bor), a broad dwarf in a good brass-buttoned coat over mail, black beard combed square. He sits in the corner with his back to the wall and his face to the room, a long oilcloth case under his chair and a brass stamp on his belt. He reads every line of the paper twice and says very little.</p><section class="secret"><h3>GM — Dugbor, factor and arbiter of the Garhammar Trade League</h3><p>MM Warrior Veteran, CR 3 (700 XP). Greatsword reskinned as a dwarven greataxe (same dice); heavy crossbow kept. Dwarf: Darkvision 120, resistance to poison. Linked, green ring, Neutral. Carries <strong>Garhammar Trade League</strong> (DC 10 History).</p><p><strong>The contract</strong> (see Zelvra): the League lends House Barrabin arms and wages against the labour of the lower cells. Dugbor is also the arbiter, so his stamp makes it binding between peoples. He chose the blind table; he knows the custom from thirty years of deep trading.</p><p><strong>The clause he is proud of.</strong> If the pledged collateral is short when called, Barrabin owes double. Three escaped sunskins on the pledge list make him <em>richer</em>. He recognises the party from the list's descriptions and decides at once that he never saw them.</p><p><strong>Wants:</strong> the seal on the paper, and the collateral short. <strong>Fears:</strong> a fight in a House bar, which voids his arbiter's neutrality.</p><p><strong>What he offers.</strong> Silence, freely. The sample case holds four League hand crossbows and a quiver of bolts. He will sell them on credit "payable at any League hall" (<strong>DC 13 Persuasion</strong>; a League man sells you the road out and sells the road to whoever chases you, so he also remembers which way they went). Asked about a way out, he says "the keepers' tunnels, if the old woman will open them", meaning Borgra.</p><p><strong>If it goes wrong:</strong> he overturns the table and holds the corner with the axe. He will not fight for Zelvra and lets her burn the paper; his copy is the dwarf-rune one, which he keeps.</p></section>`,
